th  life-threatening illnesses  farming regions will be at tremendous  risk of crop failure as local weather  behaves erratically or changes  completely  in the developing world crop failure and  drought will combine to create  massive water and food shortages  resulting in unspeakable death tolls and  the worsening of the world's ongoing  poverty and migrant crises  sooner or later oil will cease to be the  most valuable resource to imperialist  nations  it will be replaced by water when the  most basic necessity of life becomes a  target for military action and  occupation  we will begin to see suffering unlike  the human race has ever seen  but this video isn't about all the  specific details of climate fallout  i've covered that topic in previous  videos if you're interested  what we really need to talk about is how  the current dominant economic system  presents an  insurmountable obstacle to mitigating  climate disaster  so let's take a look at a few of the  actions as determined by the ipcc  which must be taken immediately or at  least in the very near future  according to the ipcc's climate and land  report  we need to implement comprehensive early  warning systems for extreme weather  as well as other observational programs  to monitor land use and degradation  biodiversity and the spread of  disease-bearing insects  we need to fund programs that will  encourage sustainable land management  which will prevent desertification and  famine we need to invest in land  restoration  bringing damaged or endangered soil and  biomes back from the brink  we must drastically reduce greenhouse  gas emissions across all sectors  including oil and gas agriculture  transportation and leisure  and we need to do all of these things in  just a few years to prevent global  catastrophe  now let's consider the nature of the  roadblock that is capitalism  to offer a simple definition capitalism  is an economic system in which the means  of production are controlled by private  owners and corporations  the primary driver of capitalism is the  profit motive  the desire for ever-increasing profits  and the accumulation of capital  it should be obvious that the very  premise of capitalism is antithetical to  any kind of long-term planning  if a system demands greater profits  every year if it demands infinite growth  it will inevitably run into the very  real problem of existing on a planet  with finite resources  if we look at the decisions made by  large multinational corporations  say exxon for example it's clear that  those decisions are made  based solely on the amount of short-term  profit they will produce  back in the 70s when exxon was at the  forefront of climate research  their scientists determined just how  detrimental fossil fuels are to the  planet  instead of making this information  public and working with policymakers to  find a solution  they marked those documents confidential  and launched a decades-long propaganda  campaign to sow doubt on the effects of  greenhouse gas emissions  by doing this they secured their  short-term profits at the expense of the  long-term survival  of millions of human beings the same is  true of giant companies like amazon  jeff bezos amazon's ceo and the richest  person on the planet  could easily afford to make amazon  employees the best paid workers in the  world  thereby securing a happy and loyal labor  force and positive public perception of  the company  instead amazon is among the worst places  to work in the country  we routinely hear horror stories about  how employees have to pee in bottles  because they'll be fired if they take a  bathroom break  or how they've had to ignore a co-worker  who had died on the floor  reducing overhead by exploiting workers  yields greater short-term profits  but it turns the public against the  company causes massive turnover  and damages their own long-term survival  by ensuring its workers have neither the  time nor the disposable income to buy  their products  this kind of behavior is common among  capitalist companies  including many smaller ones today it's  exceptionally rare to find a job that  pays a reasonable salary or offers  benefits of any kind  the name of the game is reducing costs  to maximize profits  that is the only goal of capitalism so  if we can't count on capitalism to even  take care of the workers that keep the  system running  how can we expect it to make provisions  for people on the other side of the  world  or who haven't even been born yet the  scientific community  knows that nothing will get done unless  there's a profit to be made so  their reports often contain return on  investment timelines and estimates  but if you look at their numbers there's  no way an oil executive or cruise line  ceo would go for it  sustainable land management programs  that might be profitable in 10 years  the crews and oil industries are  incredibly profitable now  why would they take a reduction in  profits when those profits are their  sole motivating factor  and if the big lobbies won't go for it  neither will the politicians who rely on  their donations  the most damaging industries also just  so happen to have the largest political  lobbies they make tremendous profits  from exploiting workers at home and  abroad  not to mention the limited resources of  the earth itself and they're willing to  shell out tens of millions of dollars to  fund politicians and propaganda  campaigns so they can keep doing it  even if you're inclined to be optimistic  and hope that these people will have a  change of heart  just look at the historical record when  multinational companies set up shop in  central america  they made enormous profits they could  have paid their local workers a good  wage  or at least a living wage instead they  were little more than slaves  the workers were brutalized by corporate  paramilitary groups  they had their families threatened if  they weren't productive enough  some parents even received the severed  hands of their children if they didn't  meet their required quota  if these people were willing to maim or  even kill their workers  why would we assume they would be  willing to make massive concessions to  help mitigate climate fallout  to take a more recent example i live in  texas the temperature is the lowest  they've seen in decades  more than 620 000 waking up without  power in texas this morning  during the recent winter storm that  knocked out the state's power grid  it became clear that deregulation and  rampant cost cutting were a major part  of the problem  texas has its own electrical grid  separate from the country's two primary  grids  the goal was to completely deregulate  the texas energy market to free it from  the evils of bureaucracy  the end result as we should expect was  rampant corruption  price gouging and cost cutting officials  decided not to take precautionary  measures like winter proofing our  electrical infrastructure  because of this millions of texans lost  power had no access to clean drinking  water  and some even froze to death in their  own homes  this example is perhaps even more  damning than the previous one  because it deals specifically with  infrastructure the majority of climate  mitigation measures will be investments  in infrastructure  if these critical projects are avoided  simply as a way to cut costs  people will die and unfortunately i  think this negligent behavior is what we  need to expect from private corporations  and corporate owned politicians  the only goal of capitalism the only  thing that matters  is profit profits must increase every  year  the entire history of capitalism is the  history of the exploitation of working  people  especially by multinational corporations  and the us military operating in the  global south  climate change is shaping up to be the  single most expensive challenge humanity  has ever faced  the world is relying on the wealthiest  nations those that disproportionately  contribute to emissions and consumption  to lead the way in fighting climate  change as we've seen in the last decade  there hasn't been a single  administration willing to take steps  bold enough to meet the challenge  all we've seen is politicians paying lip  service to establish science while  peddling woefully insufficient policies  that are rarely acted upon  if we want to ensure a livable future  for the human race  we must move past capitalism capitalism  is incapable of solving the problems it  creates  it is entirely beholden to the profit  motive and no amount of flowery language  greenwashing or reform will ever change  that  if you'd like to learn more about the  specifics of capitalism and its  relationship to the climate crisis  i highly recommend you check out our  changing climates companion video  it's a great crash course on the history  of capitalist exploitation of the people  and the planet if you enjoyed this video  consider dropping a like  if you hated it a thumbs down you can  check out my previous videos by clicking  the links on your screen  thanks for watching and i'll see you  next week  you

